kantNativeT -- SEQ 671 (Tony's ruling of 2026-11-16), the kant jitter's step 3b: native arithmetic, values in the fields.
After krResolve has marked an action, kwCompile writes a top-level statement natively when it is an = into a local
whose right side is, all the way down, a marked + - or *, a literal, or a local already written by an earlier top-level
statement. Native code reaches each field by position (irAt, fetched once at the top of the function), reads it with
irCountOf or irNumberOf, computes with the operator's own IR text (irCOUNT / irNUMBER in incant/setup), and writes the
result back through irSetCount or irSetNumber, which are opAssign. Every other statement is the testOP call of step 2c.
pop.sh runs this file twice: as written, and with the top-level kwCompile( and kwRan( lines deleted (the twin, all
interpreted). Stdout from KN ROW1 to KN END must be equal.
    ROW1   nj4: three native statements and the print. KN RAN nj4 4 testOP 1 -- testOP was called only for the print.
    ROW2   nn, every operand a number: 4.75; KN RAN nn 4 testOP 1.
    ROW3   nx: a written natively, read by the print (testOP); a written inside the if (testOP); a read natively by b = a + 1.
    ROW5a  nt called twice; ROW5b nr calls itself, with native statements before and after the call: locals as the twin.
    ROW4   KN LISTEN: lsSrc has a listener. An interpreted = fires it once; irSetCount on the same field, called from a
           hand-written text, fires it once. The row is at the helper, not in a walked action: a native statement
           writes only locals, and a local cannot carry a listener (listenTo names a field on the search list).
    ROW7   the text between KN TEXT BEGIN and KN TEXT END is nj4's, pinned in genLadder/kantNativeT.nj4.ll.
